1.106.m:
Brahmā said:
Again, hear this from me as I speak, O highly fortunate one;
the supreme greatness of the tithis was declared by the bird to the gods.

Likewise speak of the northern solstice, which is praised for the worship of Bhāskara,
where Bhānu, when worshipped, becomes the destroyer of all sins;
tell me that diligently, asked with devotion, [and] the imperishable fruit.

1.109.m:
Brahmā said:
I shall describe to you another Mārtaṇḍasaptamī, O sinless Kṛṣṇa;
hear, O hero, with a single mind as I speak of that which bestows auspiciousness.

1.115.m:
Kauśalyā said:
The Sun, who is sacrifice and the lord of sacrifice, was once pleased by us two;
this attainment of heaven is the excellent fruit of that deed.

1.120.m:
Viṣṇu said:
Those men who have not worshipped the sun are sharers in sorrow;
how may they become free from disease, free from fevers, and rid of sin?

lamp-wicks and offerings, and whatever is dear to oneself—
that man performs sacrifices daily through the worship of the sun. / The Blessed One, the sun, is the lord of sacrifice, and he is pleased even by sacrifices;
sacrifices that require abundant implements and an array of various provisions / are obtained by wealthy men, not by those with small accumulations.

1.123.m:
Śatānīka said:
Tell again this narrative that concerns the sun;
I do not reach satisfaction hearing this story, O sage.

You are Time, the creator, the destroyer, the protector, and the lord;
rivers, oceans, mountains, lightning, and Indra's bows,
dissolution and origin, manifest and unmanifest, eternal.

[The gods said:]
'Because of your excessive splendour, no one can endure your form, O omnipresent one;
let this be bearable, for the benefit of the universe, O lord!'
